// A function declared `: Nil` produces no value, so returning one is an error.
//
// This case was left open by the first half of BUGS #155 and could not be closed
// without the FunDecl.ret_type migration to AST.Type. The reason is worth keeping:
// the parser filled an OMITTED return annotation with the string "Nil", the same
// spelling a real `: Nil` declaration produced. So a check keyed to "Nil" could
// not fire on `fun f(): Nil { return 42 }` without also firing on every one of the
// hundreds of unannotated functions in the compiler's own source — and the checker
// type-checks that source, so a false positive there breaks the bootstrap outright.
//
// With `ret_type` a node, an omitted annotation is `UnknownType` and a declared
// one is `NilType`. Two distinct facts, two distinct values, and the check becomes
// expressible. Its companion `test/pass/return_type_valid.sf` pins the other side:
// an unannotated function returning a value must still be accepted.
//
// Note this check is separate from `scalar_mismatch`, which allows Nil on either
// side deliberately — for every OTHER position, Nil-vs-concrete is the nullable
// check's business rather than a scalar comparison's.

// A concrete scalar returned from a `: Nil` function.
fun nil_returns_int(): Nil {
    return 42
}

// Same, with a String — the check is on the declaration, not the value's type.
fun nil_returns_string(): Nil {
    return "value"
}

fun main() {
    nil_returns_int()
}

main()
